1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,157
Cross-validation picks a model's settings using only the training data.

2
00:00:05,157 --> 00:00:10,226
The bootstrap measures how uncertain a number is by recomputing it on resampled

3
00:00:10,226 --> 00:00:10,547
data.

4
00:00:10,547 --> 00:00:15,489
Each shows up in a handful of true or false questions, plus one tricky table.

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:22,003
In k-fold cross-validation, you split the training set into k folds.

6
00:00:22,003 --> 00:00:22,941
Here, k is 5.

7
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:27,138
For each fold, train on the other k minus 1 folds,

8
00:00:27,138 --> 00:00:29,462
and score on the one you held out.

9
00:00:29,462 --> 00:00:33,154
Every observation is used for validation exactly once.

10
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,305
Average the k scores.

11
00:00:35,305 --> 00:00:39,526
Repeat that for every candidate setting, like every value of cp,

12
00:00:39,526 --> 00:00:40,978
and keep the best one.

13
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:45,032
The cost is k model fits per setting,

14
00:00:45,032 --> 00:00:48,629
and leave one out cross-validation is just k equals n.

15
00:00:48,629 --> 00:00:53,892
With a thousand training points, ten-fold needs 10 fits and leave one out needs

16
00:00:53,892 --> 00:00:57,357
1,000, so ten-fold is about a hundred times cheaper.

17
00:00:57,357 --> 00:00:59,755
That's true on practice midterm two.

18
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:03,338
Practice midterm three lists three claims.

19
00:01:03,338 --> 00:01:05,887
Bigger k means more computation: true.

20
00:01:05,887 --> 00:01:08,168
k equals n is leave one out: true.

21
00:01:08,168 --> 00:01:11,321
k equals 1 is the validation set method: false.

22
00:01:11,321 --> 00:01:14,542
With one fold, there's nothing left to train on.

23
00:01:14,542 --> 00:01:18,768
The validation set method is a single train and validate split.

24
00:01:18,768 --> 00:01:21,183
So the answer is only the first two.

25
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:27,574
A classic false statement: if you used cross-validation to choose the final model,

26
00:01:27,574 --> 00:01:31,009
you don't need a test set, because the model can't overfit.

27
00:01:31,787 --> 00:01:36,337
Choosing the best of many settings can overfit the cross-validation scores

28
00:01:36,337 --> 00:01:37,013
themselves.

29
00:01:37,013 --> 00:01:41,932
So keep a test set, and use it once, at the end, for the final, honest estimate.

30
00:01:43,533 --> 00:01:46,252
The bootstrap starts from the data you have.

31
00:01:46,252 --> 00:01:50,514
One bootstrap sample draws n rows from your n rows, with replacement.

32
00:01:51,287 --> 00:01:54,759
So some rows show up twice or more, and others don't show up at all.

33
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:00,411
On average, about 37 percent of the rows are left out of each bootstrap sample.

34
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,878
To put a confidence interval on a trained model's test metric,

35
00:02:05,878 --> 00:02:08,442
train the model once on the training set.

36
00:02:08,442 --> 00:02:13,320
Then resample the test set B times, and recompute the metric on each resample.

37
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:15,134
The model is never retrained.

38
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:20,135
That's answer C on practice midterm two, question fourteen.

39
00:02:20,135 --> 00:02:23,349
And on midterm three: with B equal to 10,000,

40
00:02:23,349 --> 00:02:26,706
the procedure computes the metric 10,000 times.

41
00:02:26,706 --> 00:02:27,064
True.

42
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:30,803
From the B values you get three things.

43
00:02:30,803 --> 00:02:34,398
The standard error is their standard deviation.

44
00:02:34,398 --> 00:02:38,988
The bias estimate is their average minus the original value.

45
00:02:38,988 --> 00:02:44,418
And a 95 percent interval runs from the 2.5th to the 97.5th percentile.

46
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:51,373
Practice midterm two's question sixteen gives bias estimates for OSR squared and

47
00:02:51,373 --> 00:02:55,244
mean absolute error, with B of 10,000, and then a million.

48
00:02:55,244 --> 00:03:00,784
The key fact: random noise in a bootstrap estimate shrinks like one over the square

49
00:03:00,784 --> 00:03:01,451
root of B.

50
00:03:01,451 --> 00:03:05,589
A hundred times more samples means about ten times less noise.

51
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:13,091
OSR squared's bias barely moves, from 0.0300 to 0.0297.

52
00:03:13,091 --> 00:03:17,134
It's stable, so the bias is real.

53
00:03:17,134 --> 00:03:25,097
MAE's bias collapses from 0.223 to 0.009, so it was mostly noise.

54
00:03:25,853 --> 00:03:31,636
So the answer is D: MAE is likely unbiased, and OSR squared is likely biased.

55
00:03:31,636 --> 00:03:36,367
That matches theory: MAE is a plain average, which is unbiased,

56
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:40,272
while OSR squared is a ratio, which generally isn't.

57
00:03:41,867 --> 00:03:42,517
To recap.

58
00:03:42,517 --> 00:03:48,222
In k-fold cross-validation, you train on k minus 1 folds and score the held-out

59
00:03:48,222 --> 00:03:50,100
fold, k times per setting.

60
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:55,661
Leave one out is k equals n, and k equals 1 is not the validation set method.

61
00:03:55,661 --> 00:04:00,933
Cross-validation doesn't make overfitting impossible, so keep a test set.

62
00:04:00,933 --> 00:04:04,471
A bootstrap sample draws n rows with replacement.

63
00:04:04,471 --> 00:04:08,010
To get a confidence interval for a trained model,

64
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:10,899
resample the test set and never retrain.

65
00:04:10,899 --> 00:04:14,365
And a bias that holds steady as B grows is real.

66
00:04:14,365 --> 00:04:15,521
Next: ensembles.
